<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Mirdita</h1><p class="st-meta">1921 CE – 1921 CE · capital Orosh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived breakaway state of the Catholic Mirdita highlands in northern Albania, backed by Yugoslavia in 1921.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirdita">Mirdita</a> clan region in northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Albania</a> had long enjoyed autonomy under its hereditary captains. Opposing the new government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a>, the clan leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjon-marka-gjoni">Gjon Marka Gjoni</a>, with backing from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia</a>, proclaimed the Republic of Mirdita in July 1921, the declaration being made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> on Yugoslav territory.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic existed mainly as an armed revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirdita">Mirdita</a> tribesmen, supplied and armed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, which hoped to weaken <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Albania</a> and claim border areas. It appealed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> for recognition, but had no institutions beyond the clan leadership.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Albanian government forces, with commanders including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zog-i">Ahmet Zogu</a>, crushed the revolt in November 1921, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conference-of-ambassadors">Conference of Ambassadors</a> confirmed Albania's 1913 borders, ending Yugoslav claims. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjon-marka-gjoni">Gjon Marka Gjoni</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and later reconciled with the government.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1921 CE – 1921 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:97.93%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1921 CE – 1921 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Orosh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gjon Marka Gjoni</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mirdita Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1921&amp;focus=republic-of-mirdita" aria-label="Republic of Mirdita on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5168 687 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Mirdita lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5488 943L5488 850M5488 943L5475 960"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5488 850h0M5475 960h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5488 943h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5488 943h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Orosh. <a href="/atlas?year=1921&amp;focus=republic-of-mirdita">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

